{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# RCA (rebar-debug Phase 1) — MEASURED: the post-bf31 run is NOT a loop. It is genuine breadth against a FLAT budget, and there is no forced landing.\n\nFourth exhaustion, reproduced under my own instrumentation on `origin/main` @ `85805e02f1`\n(the commit that carries bf31's `search_files` fix). Every claim below is measured, not inferred.\n\n## 1. This ticket's own framing is REFUTED: no new cycle forms\n\nThe comment above predicted \"a DIFFERENT unproductive cycle now forms\". It does not.\nI re-ran `verify_completion(e369, graph=False, source=\"attested\", ref=\"HEAD\")` with every agent\ntool call logged (trace: `/tmp/c827-trace.jsonl`, 258 rows) and compared it to bf31's pre-fix trace\n(`/tmp/bf31-trace-evidence.jsonl`, 264 rows):\n\n| | bf31 run (pre-fix) | THIS run (post-fix) |\n|---|---|---|\n| tool calls to death | 264 | **258** |\n| distinct call signatures | 135 (51%) | **238 (92%)** |\n| novelty, first block | 94-98% | 100% |\n| novelty, **final** block | **0%** (calls 199-264) | **93.8% / 100%** |\n| longest repeated signature | **x37** | **x6** |\n| \"next 20 calls all repeats\" | call #121 | **never** |\n| wall clock | 1074s | 1043s |\n\n**bf31's fix worked.** The closed 4-call cycle is gone; novelty stays 72-100% through the *last*\nblock. The verifier is doing genuine, non-repetitive evidence gathering (201 `search_files`, 46\n`read_file`, 6 `list_directory`, 5 `show_ticket`) and simply runs out of TURNS.\n\nThat is exactly the position [rebar:a89d-4dd2-f421-417a] argued from its own instrumentation\n(`requests=240 tool_calls=475 distinct=451 max_consecutive_repeat=1` — \"It is NOT a runaway loop\").\na89d is **confirmed for the post-bf31 world** and should be read as live, not superseded.\n\n**The defect statement is unchanged by this, and that is the point** (see §3): under *either*\nframe — loop or breadth — the run is killed at the exact moment a tool-free turn would have\nproduced a verdict.\n\n## 2. PROVEN ROOT CAUSE\n\n**The primary completion-verify agent step is dispatched with no forced-landing boundary, so\nbudget exhaustion kills the run instead of landing it.**\n\nThe causal chain, each link cited and each dynamically confirmed:\n\n1. `build_agent_request` (`src/rebar/llm/workflow/runs.py:327-339`) constructs the `RunRequest`\n   for every workflow agent step and **never sets `tool_step_limit`**; the v3 step schema is\n   closed, so `completion-verification.yaml`'s `decide@then/verify` step has no way to supply one.\n2. The shipped forced-landing primitive is therefore dormant for the one step that needs it:\n   `agent_call.py:64-73` (`if req.tool_step_limit is not None and tools:` -> toolsets rewritten to\n   `.filtered(lambda ctx,_: ctx.run_step <= limit)`). Its ONLY production setter is the bounded\n   recovery sub-call (`completion_recovery.py:471`, `_EVIDENCE_TOOL_STEPS=16`). Current behaviour is\n   pinned by a test: `tests/unit/workflow/test_completion_token_recovery.py:132`\n   `assert runner.requests[-1].tool_step_limit is None`.\n3. The budget is FLAT: `build_usage_limits` (`structured_run.py:399-412`) sets\n   `request_limit = max(1, ceil(eff_max_iter/2))` = **240** at the default `max_iterations=480`,\n   and `tool_calls_limit = max(8, eff_max_iter)` = 480. Neither scales with ticket size or\n   criteria count.\n4. Exceeding it raises pydantic-ai `UsageLimitExceeded`; `interpret_failure`\n   (`structured_run.py:75-101`) computes a repetition diagnostic, attaches it to\n   `budget_err.diagnostic`, and raises **`LLMRunnerError`**.\n5. `CompletionAgentStep.run` (`completion_recovery.py:429-432`) catches ONLY\n   `UnretryableOutputError` and only when `_is_token_exhaustion` holds. Per `errors.py:29-46`,\n   `LLMRunnerError` is a strict **ancestor** of `StructuredOutputError` -> `UnretryableOutputError`,\n   so the clause can never match.\n6. `failure_diagnostic` stays `None`, so `raise_completion_workflow_failure`\n   (`completion_recovery.py:378-402`) skips `emit_gate_error` and raises a bare `LLMError` at\n   `:400` — no verdict, no `gate_error_v1` sidecar, diagnostic discarded one frame from the surface.\n\n### Discriminating experiments (deterministic, `FunctionModel` + `model_override`, seconds not minutes)\n\n**E1 — does withdrawing tools actually force a landing under the pydantic-ai structured stack?**\nA model that unconditionally calls a tool whenever any tool is offered; `mode=\"structured\"`,\n`output_schema=\"completion_verdict\"`:\n\n* **Arm A** (`tool_step_limit=None` — the production shape): `LLMRunnerError: agent exceeded its\n  step budget`, 20 requests / 20 tool calls, diagnostic `tool_calls_distinct: 1,\n  max_consecutive_repeat: 20`. **NO verdict.**\n* **Arm B** (`tool_step_limit=6`): `tools_offered` per request `[6,6,6,6,6,6,0]` ->\n  **a valid `completion_verdict` returned** (`verdict=FAIL`, 1 finding) in 7 requests.\n\nThis kills the objection recorded on [rebar:9a08-614d-a9d4-4a81], whose prototype withdrew tools at\n`run_step > 16` and found \"the exhausted agent emitted unparseable prose\". Under\n`mode=\"structured\"` the Agent is constructed with `output_type` (`structured_run.py:191-196`), so\nthe landing turn is schema-constrained. The half 9a08 discarded now works.\n\n**E2 — end-to-end through the REAL gate.** The same looping model injected via\n`verify_completion(ticket, graph=False, runner=PydanticAIRunner(cfg, model_override=...))`\nreproduced the production surface EXACTLY: `LLMError: completion verification workflow did not\nproduce a verdict: step 'decide@then/verify' failed: agent exceeded its step budget\n(max_iterations=480 …)`, with **`diagnostic = null`**, **`emit_gate_error` called 0 times**, 240\nmodel requests. Links 4-6 confirmed at runtime, not by reading source.\n\n**E3 — the live 258-call run** (§1) confirms link 3 against the real model: death at the request\nceiling with 92% distinct signatures.\n\n## 3. The size dependence — measured, and it is NOT descendant count\n\nThe operator's question (\"why do 19-descendant epics close and this 59-descendant one not?\").\nMeasured across all 205 epics in the store:\n\n* **Descendant count does NOT drive the verifier's context.** The close gate passes `graph=False`,\n  so `assemble_context` inlines the ticket's OWN description + comments and nothing else\n  (`gate_ops.py`, `operations.assemble_context`). Counter-example: `6001` has **39 descendants** and\n  a **2,194-char** context. The 59 descendants enter only as ~88s of deterministic pre-LLM CPU\n  (`child_closure_findings` + `epic_bug_floor_findings`), never as tool calls — consistent with\n  bf31's \"only 5 `show_ticket` calls\".\n* **`assemble_context` has NO cap and NO truncation** on the primary path. The\n  `_MAX_CONTEXT_CHARS = 100_000` at `completion_recovery.py:65` is a **recovery precondition that\n  REFUSES** (`:169` raises `CompletionRecoveryError`), not a truncator, and it is not on the primary\n  path at all. **The \"context is silently truncated, so the verifier hunts for what was dropped\"\n  chain is REFUTED** — nothing is dropped. (e369 sits at 99,347 vs that 100,000 refusal bound —\n  653 chars of headroom — which matters for [rebar:fd84-92b4-01ba-4911], not for this failure.)\n* **Context size at signature time, for every epic that carries a `completion-verifier` signature:**\n\n  | epic | gate signature | signed | criteria | context @ sign |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|\n  | 5fd2 | completion-verifier: PASS | 2026-06-24 | n/a | **169,463** |\n  | e0e2 | completion-verifier: PASS | 2026-06-25 | 9 | **131,519** |\n  | e68d | completion-verifier: PASS | 2026-07-12 | 15 | 19,568 |\n  | 86ac | completion-verifier: PASS | 2026-07-19 | 10 | 10,056 |\n  | a492 | completion-verifier: PASS | 2026-07-30 | 3 | 2,031 |\n  | 2f4c | completion-verifier: PASS | 2026-08-04 | 9 | 3,718 |\n  | **e369** | **plan-review only** | — | **14** | **95,139** |\n\n  **Context size alone does not predict failure** — 5fd2 (169k) and e0e2 (131k) passed this gate.\n  But both were **June**. Since 2026-07-01 the largest context to pass is **19,568** (e68d), and\n  e369 at 95,139 is **~5x** that. I record this as a strong correlation over a 5-run post-June\n  sample, **not** a proven mechanism; I did not establish what changed between late June and July.\n* **The honest size statement:** the budget is FLAT (240 requests, independent of ticket size or\n  criteria count) while the evidence-gathering workload scales with the ticket's volume of\n  code-grounded verifiable claims. e369's 14 criteria span two adapter trees with per-symbol and\n  per-import assertions; the trace shows that costing 258 genuine tool calls. Smaller epics finish\n  inside the flat budget; e369 does not. This is the same gap `99e22e4c23` (ticket\n  [rebar:d59e-e042-2b94-4b70]) already named as follow-up work: *\"its live blocker is the primary\n  verifier's flat step budget, which does not scale with criteria count.\"*\n* **Larger epics DID historically bypass this gate — confirmed.** `1d96` (21 descendants) and\n  `bf50` (19 descendants) each carry an explicit audit comment: *\"FORCE_CLOSE: close gate(s)\n  bypassed by user approval — no completion/signature attestation was signed.\"* **20 of 205 epics\n  carry a FORCE_CLOSE audit comment.** So \"the gate has never verified an epic this large\" is not\n  luck — the historical exit for large epics has been the unattested one this project forbids.\n\n## 4. History — this class was worked SIX times, never at the loop-breaking layer\n\n* [rebar:c7c5-12ea-ad68-4690] (the epic that CREATED the verifier) was itself **force-closed**, with\n  *\"a monolithic whole-epic LLM verification exceeds the practical step budget (a documented\n  limitation)\"*. The gate shipped with this as an accepted limitation on day one.\n* [rebar:7c58-5b15-f302-49db] listed *\"Stronger budget steering (hard tool-call cap)\"* as a\n  candidate and **explicitly deferred it**.\n* [rebar:b2bb-2ac7-5ce3-4412]'s plan review advised *\"a per-op floor for the agentic verifier\n  instead of raising the GLOBAL default\"* — **explicitly rejected as out of scope**.\n* Budget ceilings were raised **five times** instead (`c48162ca6b` 25->50, `50490e20a3`\n  `_VERIFY_MIN_STEPS` 240->480 — self-described *\"interim, blind-ish bump\"*, `98011a3f29` 50->250).\n* [rebar:9a08-614d-a9d4-4a81] (`dcc90ced45`, Gerrit 985) **built `tool_step_limit`** — comment\n  verbatim: *\"Remove all tools after this pydantic-ai run step, leaving a final turn that can\n  summarize gathered evidence as text… Executable convergence boundary.\"* It was built for the\n  recovery sub-calls only. **No commit anywhere considers and rejects applying it to the primary\n  verify step — it was never wired, not deliberately excluded.**\n* [rebar:a89d-4dd2-f421-417a] (`5f00bf5253`) built the repetition DIAGNOSTIC, explicitly\n  report-only: *\"The question 'is this a runaway or a big job?' was therefore unanswerable.\"*\n  Nothing consumes `max_consecutive_repeat` to intervene.\n* [rebar:59bc-b9ba-021a-435f] (`964791dd8f`) shipped a budget-exhaustion **recovery for\n  plan-review** — a verify step that exhausts producing an outcome rather than an exception. Direct\n  structural precedent, **scoped to plan-review only**; the completion path has no equivalent.\n\n**Detector built, remedy not wired — confirmed, and it is not a regression.** The detector (a89d)\nand the breaker (9a08) were built ~3 days apart for different purposes and never joined. The\n`docs/llm-framework.md:707-712` wording (*\"a high recursion limit means you're paying for a loop,\nfix the loop\"*) traces to `76af85ba19` (2026-06-18), which shipped `RunRequest.output_strategy=\n\"extract\"` — a genuine tool-LESS structured-extraction landing — and live-validated it. That\nmechanism was **deleted** in the LangChain->pydantic-ai cutover (`2e9bd4205f`, 2026-06-22) and the\ndoc was rewritten (`88ac13a210`) to assert `mode=\"structured\"` inherits the property — *\"the finding\ncarries over to the pydantic-ai runtime\"*, an assertion, not a re-measurement. Note for the record:\n5fd2 and e0e2 passed on 06-24/06-25, i.e. **after** that removal, so the removal alone does not\nexplain the current failure; I am NOT claiming a clean regression there.\n\n## 5. Legitimacy: `incomplete-implementation` (unlocks a repair)\n\nThe capability was designed, built, tested and given one consumer; the primary consumer was never\nwired. Cited authority:\n* `agent_call.py:64-73` + `tests/unit/test_agent_call_seam.py:90-144` — the mechanism ships with\n  tests.\n* `docs/llm-framework.md:707-712` — the project's stated invariant that a step budget is not the\n  lever.\n* `99e22e4c23` ([rebar:d59e-e042-2b94-4b70]) names the gap as follow-up work in-tree.\n* The operator-facing message the code emits — *\"Raise REBAR_LLM_MAX_STEPS or narrow the task\"*\n  (`structured_run.py:95-99`) — advises machinery that sits one `except` clause away and is\n  unreachable.\n\n## 6. Fix shape (NOT yet implemented — this ticket is unclaimed)\n\n**Site: (a) set `tool_step_limit` on the primary verify step.** Rejected alternatives:\n* **(b) make bounded recovery reachable for `LLMRunnerError`** ([rebar:fd84-92b4-01ba-4911]) —\n  engages only *after* 240 requests are burned, then re-pays N criteria x 40 iterations; e369 sits\n  653 chars under its `_MAX_CONTEXT_CHARS` refusal bound; and a89d records its\n  `_EVIDENCE_OUTPUT_TOKENS=4096` clamp truncating evidence into **false-negative FAILs**. Larger\n  behavioural change, known false-FAIL record. Leave to fd84.\n* **(c) a runner-level default** — widest blast radius on a runtime every gate shares, and it\n  collides with `runner.py:531`, which uses `req.tool_step_limit is not None` as a proxy for \"this\n  is a bounded-evidence *text* call\".\n\n**Seam:** a `with:` input on the YAML step, threaded through `build_agent_request`. This is the\nestablished extension point, stated inline at `runs.py:165-166`: *\"Carried as a `with:` input (not\na step field) because the v3 step schema is closed/immutable\"* — precedent `step_budget_per_item`,\n`output_tokens_per_item`, `output_budget: model_max` (a **sentinel, not a number** — the shape AC#3\nwants), `temperature`. Note this would be the first such knob that must reach `RunRequest` rather\nthan `cfg`.\n\n**Derivation (AC#3), single-sourced off `structured_run.py:407`:**\n\n    tool_step_limit = max(0, req_limit - LANDING_RESERVE)     # LANDING_RESERVE = 1\n\n-> 239 at `max_iterations=480`, 24 at 50, 3 at 8, 0 at 2. Measured: `run_step` allows requests\n`1..limit` and the landing is request `limit+1`, so `limit <= req_limit - 1`. Do **not** subtract\n`OUTPUT_RETRIES`: the PROMPTED retry loop calls `agent.run_sync` afresh per attempt\n(`structured_run.py:214-216`) and each attempt gets a fresh `run_step` and a fresh request budget\n(measured: attempt 2 starts at request 8 of a 10-request budget). Use `max(0, …)` not `max(1, …)`:\nat `eff_max_iter=2`, `limit=0` lands in one tool-free turn whereas `limit=1` would still exhaust.\n\n**AC#4 (no change to a converging run) — measured.** A run converging in 3 requests is byte-identical\nat `tool_step_limit` `None` / 100 / 238: 3 requests, `tools_offered=[6,6,6]`, same `PASS`. The\npredicate at `agent_call.py:71` is unconditionally true below the limit and the tool *surface* is\npreserved by the `FunctionToolset(tools)` rewrite.\n\n### The one thing a plan here MUST NOT miss\n\n`tool_step_limit` gates **model requests** (`run_ctx.run_step`). `build_usage_limits` installs a\n**second, independent** kill path — `tool_calls_limit = max(8, eff_max_iter)` = 2x `request_limit` —\nwhich a `run_step`-keyed predicate does not bound at all. Measured sweep at\n`max_iterations=480` (`request_limit=240`, `tool_calls_limit=480`):\n\n| tool calls / request | `tool_step_limit` | outcome |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2 | 239 | OK — verdict returned (478/480 calls) |\n| **3** | 239 | **`LLMRunnerError`** — 483/480 |\n| 3 | 216 (0.9x) | **`LLMRunnerError`** |\n| 3 | 180 (0.75x) | **`LLMRunnerError`** |\n| 6 | 120 (0.5x) | **`LLMRunnerError`** |\n\n**No fraction of `request_limit` guarantees the landing**, because per-request tool-call fan-out is\nunbounded. My live run sat at 258 calls / 240 requests = **1.075**, comfortably clear; a89d measured\na real run at **475/240 = 1.979**, clearing the 480 ceiling by **2 calls**. The remedy is available\nand cheap: `run_ctx.usage.tool_calls` is live and exact inside the predicate (measured\n`(run_step, tool_calls) = [(1,0), (2,3), (3,6)]`), so the fix is a two-term predicate at\n`agent_call.py:70-71`, not a new mechanism. A fix that sets only `tool_step_limit` will pass a\n1-call-per-request test and can still die in production.\n\n**Residual risk to weigh in review:** a forced landing yields a verdict on incomplete evidence, and\n`completion_verdict` has **no INDETERMINATE** — `_norm_verdict` normalizes anything but `\"PASS\"` to\n`\"FAIL\"` (`contracts.py`; re-applied at `completion.py:358-395`). So it **fails closed** (blocks a\nclose, never certifies), and under-evidenced FAIL is already the prompt's sanctioned behaviour:\n`reviewers/completion_verifier.md:150-151` — *\"If, after a bounded search, you cannot find evidence\nthat a requirement is met, record what you searched and treat it as NOT MET — never assume.\"* The\ngenuine risk is a *quiet* false FAIL, indistinguishable from a considered one; the landing turn\nshould say so in `detail`.\n\n## 7. Can e369 close honestly once this lands?\n\n**It becomes possible for the first time, but it is not guaranteed and this fix does not promise a\nPASS.** The fix converts \"killed with no verdict, `--force-close` the only exit\" into \"a real\n`completion_verdict` on ~239 requests of genuine evidence\". Two things could still make that verdict\na FAIL, neither in this ticket's scope:\n1. e369's criterion #1 — *\"every child story is closed and its change is `Verified +1` on Gerrit\"* —\n   has **no evidence any repository tool can reach**; the deterministic child-closure proof the\n   precheck already computes (`blocking=0, uncertified=0`) is collapsed to a boolean at\n   `gate_ops.py:132` and never reaches the verifier's prompt (`completion-verification.yaml:48-56`\n   wires only `context`). This is the most likely route to a genuine FAIL.\n2. The flat budget still does not scale with criteria count ([rebar:a89d-4dd2-f421-417a],\n   [rebar:d59e-e042-2b94-4b70]), so a forced landing on a ticket this size lands on *partial*\n   evidence by construction.\n\n**e369 remains `in_progress` and untouched.** Nothing was force-closed and no status was changed.\n\nEvidence: `/tmp/c827-trace.jsonl` (258 rows, this run), `/tmp/c827-run.log`,\n`/tmp/bf31-trace-evidence.jsonl` (264 rows, bf31's pre-fix run) for the side-by-side."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785941495406353001,"uuid":"4257e343-fddf-4596-af6a-37d36d6b1842"}